*Executive summary.* When a tube preamplifier, studio console or vintage hi-fi unit fails, a technician must find which of its many parts failed; specialist shops we sampled charge \$125 to \$150 for that bench evaluation << cite('rates_studio_doctors', 'rates_archer_hifi', 'rates_cw_guitar_audio') >>. Differential helps with the search on circuits it has a model of (@fig:concept). It simulates how every cataloged part failure changes the readings, keeps a probability for every group of suspects that no reading can tell apart, and recommends the measurement expected to narrow them most per unit of effort. A language model reads the complaint and explains; every reading comes from the technician, and safety locks run in code. In a pilot on simulated units it named the group containing the failed part first on << pct(bt.T1.estimate, 1) >> of as-new units and << pct(bt.T6.estimate, 1) >> of aged ones, with << num(bt.T9.estimate, 2) >> times the effort of half-split tracing to reach a confirmed answer. Success, fixed before any test unit is run (@tab:targets), needs at least << T['T1'].pass_count >> of << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> simulated units right, << T['T6'].pass_count >> of << num(ev.aged_cases, 0) >> aged ones and << T['T7'].pass_count >> of << ev.fault_board_min >> faults inserted into real boards, plus margins over scripted procedures and a chat model. All evidence so far is simulated; hardware boards, interviews and a technician study will test transfer and benefit.

#figure(
  image("/results/figures/concept.svg", width: 80%),
  caption: [Differential in one picture. Offline, each circuit and its fault catalog are simulated with SPICE (circuit-simulation software) under part tolerances, as-new and aged, and learned as fault signatures. Online, a Bayesian loop picks each measurement; the language model (LLM) reads the complaint, proposes meter-photo readings for the technician to confirm and explains. Safety locks and the check that every number traces to a measurement run in code.],
) <fig:concept>

<< part(1, "Problem, stakeholders and industry context") >>

*The problem.* Studio consoles, tube preamplifiers and vintage hi-fi are repaired long after their makers stop supporting them, and finding the failed part is usually the slow step: for heavy equipment, a trade article puts diagnosing an electrical fault at four hours or more against a ten-minute repair << cite('conequip_electrical_diagnosis') >>; for audio, the interviews below will measure it. Three specialist shops we sampled charge \$125 to \$150 for a bench evaluation, one covering the first hour of repair and one credited against it << cite('rates_studio_doctors', 'rates_archer_hifi', 'rates_cw_guitar_audio') >>; another bills a one-hour minimum even when no fault is found << cite('rates_british_audio') >>. These are examples, not a survey.

*Size of the need.* No statistic counts audio repair alone. U.S. electrical and electronics installers and repairers held about 117,700 jobs in 2025, and the Bureau of Labor Statistics projects 2% growth to 2035, below the 3.5% average, with most openings coming from replacing workers who leave or retire << cite('bls_ooh_eeir', 'bls_ep_2025_35') >>. For the specialist niche, Stereophile's retail column reports that skilled hi-fi technicians are scarce and dwindling and that analog circuitry is not taught at two-year colleges << cite('stereophile_retales_13') >>; the interviews will test this. An AI assistant raised support agents' productivity 14% on average and 34% for novices << cite('brynjolfsson_genai_at_work_2023') >>; if that holds at the bench, newer technicians gain most, so the tool must support their judgment, not replace it.

*Where it pays.* A new circuit needs its schematic transcribed and, for our << cs.components >>-part channel strip, about << m('library.setup_cost.channel_strip.total_minutes') | round | int >> minutes of computer time; it pays fastest where one model serves many units, such as a console of identical channel strips or a popular receiver. We will time one transcription and report how many repairs a model must serve to repay it.

*Where current AI stands.* Maintenance AI retrieves documented knowledge: service histories (PTC ServiceMax AI << cite('ptc_servicemax_ai_2025') >>), manuals (Microsoft's field-service Copilot << cite('msft_d365_field_service_page') >>), maintenance guidance (Siemens << cite('siemens_maintenance_copilot_2025') >>), agentic workflows (IBM Maximo 9.2 << cite('ibm_mas_92_2026') >>) and repair guides (iFixit's FixBot << cite('ifixit_fixbot_2025') >>). None of these five simulates the circuit on the bench. The best chat model scores 95.50% on the September 2026 Razavi-Bench of textbook analog questions, as graded by another language model << cite('razavi_bench_2026') >>; at the bench, parts sit anywhere within tolerance, faults look alike, measurements cost effort and some points carry lethal voltage. A research system raised its base model from 79.52% to 97.59% on undergraduate circuit problems by adding a diagram detector and a simulation loop that re-checks answers << cite('e2e_circuit_analysis_2025') >>; Differential applies that idea, a simulator checking the reasoning, to diagnosis.

*Jurisdiction and rules.* We design for the U.S. first (the team works in New Jersey) and check EU rules for later sale. Congress has passed no AI statute that would cover such a tool, and the Administration has asked it for a national framework preempting state AI laws << cite('wh_ai_legislative_framework_2026') >>; Colorado's re-enacted AI law covers automated decisions about people, such as employment, from 1 January 2027 << cite('co_sb26_189') >>, one reason the license forbids rating staff with the tool. The EU AI Act would not class a bench aid as high-risk: no Annex I law requires it to pass third-party assessment, and no Annex III area applies unless shops rated technicians or trainees with it << cite('eu_ai_act_text_mirror') >>. Users must be told they are dealing with an AI from 2 August 2026 << cite('ec_ip_26_1714') >>; the 2026 amendments moved the Annex III rules to 2 December 2027 << cite('eu_ai_omnibus_2026_1744') >> and recast AI literacy as support for staff's literacy << cite('eu_ai_act_consolidated_2026', 'ec_ai_literacy_qa') >>, which our training material serves.

