*Interviews before M2.* Six semi-structured interviews of about 45 minutes, after an ethics-board determination: three bench technicians from independent audio shops, one trainee, one shop owner and one owner of vintage equipment, recruited through shops and audio societies rather than employers. They cover how long diagnosis takes, which faults recur, how a reading is judged "off", what a tool may and may not do, and how part removal is agreed. We will also ask one shop for 50 anonymized repair tickets to code faults and diagnosis time.

#decision("The primary user is a qualified bench technician")[
*Options:* a consumer self-repair assistant or a professional bench tool. *Choice:* professional, because diagnosis often means testing a powered, open chassis. OSHA's rules for employers require live parts to be de-energized unless testing needs power (parts under 50 V to ground excepted, if that adds no burn or arc-blast exposure) and let only qualified persons, trained in the equipment and its hazards, work on or test energized parts << cite('osha_1910_333', 'osha_1910_331_399', 'osha_1910_334') >>; << hv.hv_normal + hv.hv_under_fault >> of the channel strip's << cs.test_points >> test points can exceed 50 V (@tab:library). Self-employed technicians fall outside OSHA, so the license asks the same qualification of every user. *Consequence:* licensed to repair businesses for qualified technicians; trainees work supervised; mains-side work is refused. Hobbyists keep forums and general chat assistants, which follow content policies but have no hazard map of the unit on the bench.
]

<< part(2, "How diagnosis is done today, and the data landscape") >>

*Today's practice.* The usual sequence, which the interviews will check: confirm the complaint; look for burnt or bulging parts; check the supplies; follow a test tone stage by stage, often halving the search ("half-split tracing"); compare voltages with the manual's chart or the healthy channel; test tubes and electrolytics; then unsolder a suspect and confirm the fault clears. The hard judgment is which reading is really "off", since parts sit anywhere within tolerance and old ones drift beyond it: aging electrolytics lose capacitance and gain series resistance << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>. Replacing every electrolytic ("recapping") often cures a symptom, but it costs parts and time, risks the board and never names the failed part.

*The information is uneven.* Service manuals with schematics and voltage charts exist for many classic models, but units are often modified or undocumented. U.S. state repair laws cover newer digital products, not vintage analog gear: New York's took effect on 28 December 2023 << cite('ny_gbl_399nn', 'ny_ag_repair_2024') >>, and California, Colorado, Oregon, Washington and Texas followed with laws limited by date or product << cite('ca_prc_42488', 'co_hb24_1121', 'or_sb1596_2024', 'wa_eshb1483_2025', 'tx_hb2963_2025') >>. The EU's obligation to repair covers only the product groups in its Annex II, such as washing machines and displays << cite('eu_r2r_directive_2024_1799', 'ec_r2r_qa_2024') >>. Differential therefore needs a circuit model per product, built from the schematic; it trusts the model's hazard map only once its healthy readings match the manual's chart, and until then treats every point of a high-voltage unit as high voltage. Modified units are what "no single fault fits" is for; units without a schematic are out of scope.

*Labeled fault data is scarce, so we simulate it.* Confirming a failed part takes unsoldering and is rarely recorded; shop logs are private and, after recapping, unlabeled; manuals give only healthy values. We know of no public dataset of bench readings with confirmed failed parts; published methods use benchmark filter circuits << cite('gao_yang_jiang_2021_incipient_tim') >> and simulated data << cite('cloete_stander_wilke_2022_access') >>. The analog-test literature built simulated fault dictionaries << cite('bandler_salama_1985') >>, showed that some faults cannot be told apart at the available test points, forming ambiguity groups << cite('stenbakken_souders_stewart_1989') >>, and applied machine learning << cite('he_he_li_2020_gan_tim', 'gao_yang_jiang_2021_incipient_tim') >>; we follow that path (@tab:library). A simulated unit is one draw of every part within its tolerance, the mains within ±<< m('library.instrument.mains_tol_pct') | round | int >>%, and one cataloged fault (an open, a short, a drift, or a part-specific mode such as a leaky transistor or a worn tube). Because units that reach a bench are old, << design.simulation.aging.training_share_pct | round | int >>% of training units are also aged: electrolytics up to << m('library.aging.electrolytic_c_loss_max_pct') | round | int >>% low with up to << m('library.aging.electrolytic_esr_max_x') | round | int >>× their series resistance, resistors up to << m('library.aging.resistor_drift_max_pct') | round | int >>% high, tubes up to << m('library.aging.triode_emission_loss_max_pct') | round | int >>% weaker. Every reading carries a real instrument's error: a << m('library.instrument.dmm_input_mohm') | round | int >> MΩ meter loads each DC node << cite('fluke_117_input_characteristics') >> and reads within ±(<< num(m('library.instrument.dmm_pct'), 1) >>% + << m('library.instrument.dmm_counts') >> counts) << cite('fluke_117_dcv_accuracy') >>, an oscilloscope within ±<< num(m('library.instrument.scope_pct'), 0) >>% of full scale << cite('rigol_ds1000z_user_guide_2014') >>. The library gave << num(m('library.simulations.total_draws'), 0) >> units, << m('library.simulations.failed_draws') >> failing to converge. Hand calculations reproduce all << m('hand_calcs.rows_checked') >> healthy bias points and gains checked, each within a tolerance set beforehand (<< num(m('hand_calcs.rel_tol_min_pct'), 1) >>% to << num(m('hand_calcs.rel_tol_max_pct'), 0) >>%, or << num(m('hand_calcs.abs_tol_db'), 1) >> dB for the tone stack). For fault behavior, an audit re-solved << fa.faults >> randomly drawn faults (up to two of each fault type per circuit) without the simulator, with a solver we wrote and the published device equations; all << fa.faults_agree >> agreed within twice the meter's accuracy (95% interval << pct(fa.ci95[0], 0) >>-100%), after a first run in which 6 of 42 disagreed through the audit solver's own omissions. That checks the simulator, not reality; the boards test reality. A complaint alone is weak evidence: on the channel strip a hum complaint fits << m('library.symptoms.channel_strip.faults_per_symptom.hum') >> faults, low output << m('library.symptoms.channel_strip.faults_per_symptom.low_gain') >>, and << m('library.symptoms.channel_strip.latent_faults') >> faults usually cause none.

