*How it will be measured.* The test set holds << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> single-fault units (<< ev.channel_strip_cases >> on the channel strip, << ev.cases_per_block >> per block) and << num(ev.aged_cases, 0) >> aged units with the same mix, on seeds disjoint from training. Each case draws a fault uniformly from the catalog and keeps the unit only if it shows a symptom, else draws another, so faults count by how often they cause a symptom; old electrolytics and tubes fail more often than film resistors, so T14 also reweights to a capacitor-heavy mix. Complaints come from a phrase bank the reader was not developed on (its score was looked at once, before the rules last changed; T18 uses a sealed second bank), in three voices, never name a part, and drop each symptom with probability << m('library.complaint_noise.p_omit_pct') | round | int >>% and add each other symptom with << m('library.complaint_noise.p_add_pct') | round | int >>%. Stress sets: << ev.unmodeled_cases >> units outside the catalog (70% double faults, 30% a wrong-value part or a solder bridge) and tolerances 1.5, 2 and 3 times wider (a curve, no bar). The scripts share the engine's inference and stopping rule and unsolder their leading suspect at << pct(m('library.design.confirm_at'), 0) >> belief, so each margin isolates how measurements are chosen. A language model works alone, and with our simulator as a tool (no bar), on a stratified << ev.llm_subset >>-unit subset with the same information, three sessions per unit. For T7 a teammate who does not record inserts << ev.fault_board_min >> faults, in a sealed random order, into three 24 V driver boards (one of used parts). Study S1 will time at least six technicians with and without the tool; it is pre-registered without a bar because six people cannot show a benefit reliably.

*How the bars were set; no test unit has been run.* Bars come from a pilot on units simulated for it alone (<< m('pilot2.n_cases.channel_strip') >> channel strip and << m('pilot2.n_cases.driver') >> per block, as-new and aged; << m('bars.targets.T13.n_pilot') | int >> out-of-catalog). Weighted like the test set, the full system named the right group first on << pct(bt.T1.estimate, 1) >> of as-new and << pct(bt.T6.estimate, 1) >> of aged units: aging cost << num(((100 * bt.T1.estimate) | round(1)) - ((100 * bt.T6.estimate) | round(1)), 1) >> points, << pct(bt.T6.aged_flag_rate, 1) >> of aged units being flagged "no single fault fits" and << pct(bt.T6.aged_wrong_name_rate, 1) >> misnamed. <% set ep = eff.engine.probing | round(1) %><% set eu = (eff.engine.unsoldering + eff.engine.confirming_charge) | round(1) %><% set hp = eff.half_split.probing | round(1) %><% set hu = (eff.half_split.unsoldering + eff.half_split.confirming_charge) | round(1) %>To a confirmed answer, the engine alone spent << num(ep + eu, 1) >> effort units (<< num(ep, 1) >> probing, << num(eu, 1) >> unsoldering), half-split tracing << num(hp + hu, 1) >> (<< num(hp, 1) >> and << num(hu, 1) >>); reading the complaint changed accuracy from << pct(m('bars.context.accuracy_weighted.engine'), 1) >> (engine alone) to << pct(bt.T1.estimate, 1) >> and effort by << num(-m('bars.context.complaint_effort_saving_pct'), 1) >>%, so the complaint reader has yet to prove itself (T11, T18). Each bar is the pilot estimate moved << m('bars.k') >> standard errors against the system and rounded; T5, with no pilot possible without an API key, was fixed in advance at the smallest margin we judged would justify a per-circuit model over a general one; T7's 70% was fixed from what a pass must show, since << T['T7'].pass_count >> of << ev.fault_board_min >> is the smallest count whose exact lower bound clears it. Each primary is judged alone. If they were independent, all pilot-estimable primaries would pass with a chance of about << (100 * targets.joint_chance_primaries.chance) | round | int >>%, so a miss or two is the likely outcome, and each will be reported as one.

*Scope.* In: single static faults (opens, shorts, drift, wear) and the hum and distortion they cause, in supplies, preamplifier and line-level stages. Out: power amplifiers (another catalog), intermittent and mechanical faults (static simulation cannot produce them), double faults (flagged only), mains-side work, units without a schematic, unattended probing.

#decision("Score by ambiguity group and effort to a confirmed answer")[
*Options:* per-part accuracy and a count of measurements, or group accuracy and weighted effort to a confirmed answer. *Choice:* the latter: per-part scoring rewards lucky guesses between faults with identical readings, unsoldering a part is not like touching a probe, and an answer is worth acting on only once confirmed. *Consequence:* group sizes are reported (@tab:library), and raw effort is reported beside the confirmed effort.
]

<< part(4, "Preliminary AI approach, risks and ethics") >>

*Why a hybrid.* Language models guess when uncertain, partly because training and evaluation reward guessing << cite('kalai_why_hallucinate_2025') >>; the 97.59% system in << P1 >> needed a simulation check. Simulation supplies the physics; Bayesian inference keeps a probability for every suspect group, with "no single fault fits" as an explicit hypothesis, updated after each reading through likelihoods learned from the simulated units: one to three full-covariance Gaussians per fault, chosen by BIC, over the circuit's readings (<< cs.spice_observables >> on the channel strip, << m('library.circuits.channel_strip.observables_by_kind.dc') >> of them DC voltages), which give a likelihood for any subset of readings in closed form; "no single fault fits" has a broad flat density whose level is calibrated on out-of-catalog validation units to a << pct(m('library.design.false_alarm_budget'), 0) >> false-alarm budget; with boosted trees << cite('ke_2017_lightgbm') >> as a comparison engine (weighted pilot top-1: << pct(m('bars.context.accuracy_weighted.engine'), 1) >> and << pct(m('bars.context.engine_disc_top1_weighted'), 1) >>). The next measurement maximizes expected information gain per unit of effort, a greedy form of sequential fault diagnosis << cite('pattipati_alexandridis_1990') >>; a diagnosis stops at << pct(m('library.design.stop_threshold'), 0) >> belief in one group, at the budget, or when nothing informative remains. The language model (Anthropic's Claude, version pinned) reads complaints (offline, a rule-based reader does), proposes meter-photo readings for confirmation and explains each step; every number it writes must trace to a reading (T17). It stays only if T5, T18 and T19 show it helps.

