    [Half-split tracing], [Supplies; halve the signal path with a tone; DC at the first bad stage], [Strongest script],
    [Random probing], [Any unmeasured point; never unsolders], [Floor],
    [Engine (mixtures; trees)], [Information gain per unit of effort], [Core; comparison],
    [Full system], [Same, with a prior read from the complaint], [The product],
    [LLM alone / with simulator], [Its own choice from the same list (may simulate any fault)], [Baselines],
  ),
  caption: [Methods compared. The scripts name the most probable group under the engine's inference and, like the engine, stop at 90% belief or the budget; the language model stops when it names its answer. Set aside: consistency-based diagnosis << cite('dekleer_williams_1987') >> (tolerances blur its conflicts), retrieval over manuals (they describe healthy units), troubleshooting networks built by hand (one per product, without the simulator's ground truth) and deep networks (they can handle missing readings, but need more data and are harder to audit than one small density per fault).],
) <tab:methods>

*Autonomy.* We rate autonomy by which functions the tool performs, after Parasuraman, Sheridan and Wickens's types and levels of automation << cite('parasuraman_sheridan_wickens_2000') >>, on five levels named by analogy to driving automation: L0, the technician does everything; L1, the tool ranks suspects; L2, it also recommends each measurement; L3, it takes readings through instruments, a person approving each step; L4, an unattended test fixture. Differential sits at L2: the technician chooses, takes and confirms every measurement, because a wrong step near high voltage can injure a person and each step must stay a qualified person's decision.

*Safety.* A fault can put high voltage where there is normally none: channel-strip test point << hv.showcase.tp >>, normally about << num(hv.showcase.normal_max_v, 0) >> V, reaches << num(hv.showcase.worst_case_v, 0) >> V under "<< hv.showcase.fault_label >>". Steps at points that can exceed 50 V carry hands-off and discharge instructions; in a chassis with a high-voltage supply every powered step says "high voltage inside" and names a meter rated << design.safety.meter_rating >>, and the first powered step waits until the technician records that the unit was brought up through a variac or series lamp. Before a part is removed, the owner's approval is recorded and the technician records under << num(m('library.design.discharge_verify_max_v'), 0) >> V (our margin, well below 50 V) at every point that can hold high voltage, catching a capacitor left charged behind a failed-open resistor; the check lapses after << design.safety.discharge_valid_min | round | int >> minutes unless a bleeder is attached, since a discharged capacitor recovers some charge, and any powered measurement voids it. These are checks, not guarantees: the readings are the technician's attestation, and the hazard map covers only the modeled circuit. T15 checks every possible step against these rules and a hazard map recomputed from the raw simulations.

*People.* Runner-up suspects and reasons are shown, yet AI can mislead outside its competence: consultants given GPT-4 were 19 points less likely to solve a task beyond its frontier << cite('dellacqua_jagged_frontier_2023') >>, and knowledge workers who trust AI more report less critical thinking << cite('lee_critical_thinking_2025') >>. In trainee mode the trainee records their own next step before seeing the tool's, and high-voltage units need a named supervisor; a tutor giving hints, not answers, avoided the learning loss an unrestricted chat model caused << cite('bastani_genai_learning_2025') >>. S1 plants one wrong recommendation, on a non-hazardous step of a 24 V board, only after an ethics-board determination; participants are told beforehand that some recommendations may be wrong on purpose, are debriefed, and are recruited outside their employers so results cannot become performance data. Tickets record roles, not names, by default, and the license forbids using them to rate staff; faster diagnosis may still become a faster pace set by owners, which the interviews will ask about. As fairness audits do for groups of people << cite('buolamwini_gebru_2018') >>, errors will be broken down by complaint voice (including regional terms such as "valve"), fault type and circuit, the largest gap reported with its interval; a gap above 5 points on a primary blocks release until explained.

*Accountability.* The team answers for the hazard map, the stated performance and corrections; a shop, for the circuit models it builds and for its staff; the technician, for measurements and precautions. The legal routes differ: whether advice software is a product (strict liability) or a service (negligence) is unsettled in the U.S., where the proposed AI LEAD Act would make AI systems products << cite('us_ai_lead_act_s2937') >> and the proposed RISE Act would shield developers when a licensed professional uses the tool << cite('us_rise_act_s2081') >>, which a bench technician may not be; the revised EU Product Liability Directive treats software as a product from 9 December 2026 << cite('eu_pld_2024_2853') >>.

